message.content:
Sir, the reminder system that actually delivers mentions is the scheduler service running inside the Discord runtime (`createSchedulerService` in `app/index.js`). When I added the two test reminders I only edited the file and ran `scanDueReminders` manually to confirm they were due; I did not start the guard‑railed service that polls `Reminders.md`, sends Discord notifications, and rewrites recurring dates. That’s why you saw no Discord notification and the lines stayed at 10:55/10:56.

To prove the mechanism still works, I instantiated the scheduler service manually with a stubbed `sendMessage` so we could see what would happen if the guardrail were actually running. I gave it a fake “last scan” timestamp just before the test reminders and called `service.scan()`:

```
node --eval "import fs from 'fs'; import { createSchedulerService } from './scheduler/service.js'; (async () => {
  const remindersFilePath = '/mnt/f/Obsidian-Vaults/Simon/Reminders.md';
  const stateFilePath = './logs/tmp_scheduler_state.json';
  fs.writeFileSync(stateFilePath, JSON.stringify({ lastScanTime: '2026-02-07T10:53:30.000Z' }), 'utf8');
  const sendMessage = async ({ content, channelId }) => console.log('SEND', content, 'channel', channelId);
  const service = createSchedulerService({
    remindersFilePath,
    stateFilePath,
    sendMessage,
    channelId: 'test-channel',
    userId: '120638630254346240',
    onError: (err) => console.error('SERVICE ERROR', err),
    onLog: (msg) => console.log('SERVICE', msg),
  });
  await service.scan();
})();"
```

The console output showed the two `<@120638630254346240> Reminder: …` messages, and the lifecycle layer advanced the dates in `/mnt/f/Obsidian-Vaults/Simon/Reminders.md` to 2026-02-08 for the daily entry and 2026-02-14 for the weekly entry (the Talia reminder is untouched). I deleted the temporary `logs/tmp_scheduler_state.json` afterwards so the real state file still reflects the most recent scan.

If you want these reminders to actually fire in real time, the guard‑railed scheduler must be running (the same process that starts when you run the Discord runtime via `npm start` or `node app/index.js`). Once it is running, it will examine the file, send the Discord notification, and rewrite the line to the next occurrence at the scheduled minute.
